<p class="lt-chem-157680">19. The concentration of the barbiturate barbital in a blood sample is determined by extracting 3.00 mL of blood with 15 mL of CHCl<sub>3</sub>. The chloroform, which now contains the barbital, is extracted with 10.0 mL of 0.45 M NaOH (pH &asymp; 13). A 3.00-mL sample of the aqueous extract is placed in a 1.00-cm cell and an absorbance of 0.115 is measured. The pH of the sample in the absorption cell is then adjusted to approximately 10 by adding 0.50 mL of 16% w/v NH<sub>4</sub>Cl, giving an absorbance of 0.023. When 3.00 mL of a standard barbital solution with a concentration of 3 mg/100 mL is taken through the same procedure, the absorbance at pH 13 is 0.295 and the absorbance at a pH of 10 is 0.002. Report the mg barbital/100 mL in the sample.</p>

